## 10. API AND GITHUB INTEGRATION BOUNDARIES

Keep GitHub integration modular for later implementation.

Potential future functionality includes:

- Public repository import.
- Public pull-request retrieval.
- Base and proposed source retrieval.
- Actual diff generation.
- Repository metadata display.

Do not add private repository access, GitHub OAuth, automatic pull-request comments, or broad repository permissions to the initial MVP.

When a real GitHub integration is requested, verify the current official API documentation, supported endpoints, authentication requirements, rate limits, and permission scopes before implementation.

Do not invent repository data or show sample metadata as live information.

Keep the initial sample pull-request workflow independent of GitHub access.

## 11. SECURITY AND PRIVACY

Implement security controls appropriate to the actual architecture.

Required controls:

- Never execute uploaded source code.
- Never run uploaded scripts or install uploaded dependencies.
- Validate file extensions, file sizes, archive paths, and extracted content.
- Reject path traversal and unsafe archive entries.
- Limit archive processing and text parsing to avoid excessive memory use.
- Avoid exposing API credentials.
- Avoid logging complete source files or sensitive user content unnecessarily.
- Communicate when source code is transmitted to an external AI provider.
- Handle malformed input, model errors, rate limits, and network failures.
- Use least-privilege access for any future GitHub integration.
- Avoid persistent source-code storage unless explicitly implemented and disclosed.
- Keep demonstration data separate from uploaded user content.

Do not claim that source code remains local if the configured AI integration transmits it to an external provider.

For the initial MVP, keep uploaded content in application memory where feasible and provide a clear way to reset the current workspace.

